  "problem": "The tangent to the curve $y=k(x)$ at the point where $x=1$ intersects the $x$-axis at $(c,0).$ Find the value of $c.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We have $k(x)=3{e}^{x}-{x}^{3}-2x.$ In step 7, we found that the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=1$ is $m=3e-5.$\n\nThe $y$-coordinate of the curve at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=k(1)=3{e}^{1}-(1)^{3}-2(1)=3e-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(3e-3) & =(3e-5)(x-1) \\\\ y-3e+3 & =(3e-5)x-3e+5 \\\\ y & =(3e-5)x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $x$-intercept of this tangent line, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(3e-5)x+2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{2}{3e-5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{5-3e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(\\frac{2}{5-3e},0),$ and we have $c=\\frac{2}{5-3e}.$",
